## Train direction convention

There is no hidden quarter-turn in the phase/corridor arguments. A train's projective phase is the direction of its parallel physical rhombus edges. Its centerline is transverse, but each terminal boundary edge has the train phase direction.

Orient a physical rhombus-boundary edge with its incident retained rhombus on the left. A complete train then has opposite terminal orientations `u` and `-u`. This is the convention used by signed return.

## §104 and §§107--108

The §104 proof is valid with its stated order of limits: fix the micro/macro cutoff, take `D->infinity`, then exhaust the cutoff. Convex Hausdorff/perimeter continuity transfers tangent-window estimates, and boundary data are counted by edge occurrence, including bridges.

For a compact wrapping support with complement gaps `g_j`, the disk outside its convex hull is the union, modulo boundary measure zero, of the minor circular segments over the complement components. Hence

\[
\operatorname{area}(\operatorname{conv}S)={\pi\over4}-{1\over8}\sum_j(g_j-\sin g_j).
\]

The identity remains valid for countably many components. §§107--108 correctly use it to obtain the safe finite rhombus count `R_D>=0.472D^2` and to transport widened gap stability into the phase/corridor contradiction.

## §109 correction

For one fixed pair of distinct phase labels let `a_j,b_j` be shore sizes in bin `j` and `e_j<=a_jb_j` its simple crossing count. If every bin has one shore below `aD`, partition bins by which shore is small:

\[
X=\{j:a_j<aD\},\qquad Y=\{j:a_j\ge aD,\ b_j<aD\}.
\]

Then

\[
\sum_{j\in X}e_j\le aD\sum_{j\in X}b_j,\qquad
\sum_{j\in Y}e_j\le aD\sum_{j\in Y}a_j.
\]

Because the phase labels are distinct, the trains counted on the two right-hand sides are disjoint members of the same local train set. Therefore

\[
\sum_{j\in X}b_j+\sum_{j\in Y}a_j\le T_D\le C_TD
\]

and

\[
\boxed{\sum_j e_j\le aC_TD^2.}
\]

The older proof used the valid but nonsharp ceiling `2aC_TD^2`. Thus its theorem `a<r/(6C_T)` survives, but its claimed method limit does not. The corrected limit is `r/(3C_T)`.

## Exact train ceiling

Canonical §§82 and 89 give

\[
L_D+Q_D=4a_D-6-2s_D-2c_D,\qquad
P_{R,D}\le L_D+5Q_D,\qquad
2T_D=P_{R,D}.
\]

Hence

\[
T_D\le2a_D-3-s_D-c_D+2Q_D.
\]

Retaining the favorable §101 face currency gives

\[
C\le2\alpha-\limsup_D{2s_D+c_D+Q_D\over D},\qquad 2\alpha-C\le\sigma,
\]

so

\[
{Q_D\over D}\le\sigma+o(1),\qquad
\boxed{{T_D\over D}\le U(\theta)+2\sigma+o(1).}
\]

This argument is valid. Its first landed copy was another file labeled “§109,” which is a numbering defect rather than a proof defect.

## §116 zero-host closed-face transport

For a directed unit-edge walk `W` with displacement `z`, put `q=z/|z|` when `z!=0` and `q=0` when `z=0`. In either case

\[
\ell(W)-|z|=\sum_{e\in W}(1-q\cdot v_e).
\]

Every bounded nonquadrilateral face boundary is a closed contact cycle. Oriented with the defect face on the right, each occurrence adjacent to a strict rhombus has exactly the physical train-end orientation, while the cycle has host `0`. The occurrence-counted outer walk absorbs all remaining physical train ends.

Cut the outer walk at an attachment-cell diameter pair. The two open arcs have hosts `q,-q`; every bounded defect-face cycle has host `0`. Thus all complete trains are double-good and

\[
w_{\{q,-q,0\}}(u)=2|q\cdot u|.
\]

For the two train directions of a strict rhombus the return loss is greater than `4-2sqrt(3)`. Area transport along a complete train is bounded by the attachment-cell diameter `d`, yielding

\[
\boxed{d(L+P_{exc}-2d)\ge(4-2\sqrt3)A_4.}
\]

The face ledger gives `P_exc=4f_exc+Q` and `L+Q=4a-6-2s-2c`, so `Q` cancels and gives the exact face-count form recorded above. This removes bounded defect-face ends from the uncontrolled bad-endpoint population; the unresolved quantity is the outer-walk detour.

## §117 whole-area signed return

For actual directed routes with hosts `q_j`, injectively assign good physical train endpoints to matching route occurrences. For double-good trains,

\[
E\ge\sum_\tau(2-w_Q(u_\tau)).
\]

With at most three unit hosts, two train directions crossing at projective angle greater than `pi/3` have combined return loss at least `2-sqrt(3)`. Summing over good-good rhombi gives

\[
dE\ge(2-\sqrt3)A_{gg}.
\]

The diffuse-bad-train estimate

\[
A_{gg,D}\ge A_{4,D}-2T_DM_{bad}(\epsilon)-{\Delta_D\over\epsilon}
\]

is valid with the required order `epsilon fixed -> D->infinity -> epsilon->0`. The hostile audit's proof-only `sigma<=0.0029` window is sound.

## §118 correlated slack and sharp companion

Canonical §101 gives the exact nonnegative decomposition

\[
\boxed{\sigma=x+y+z+w}
\]

with `x=U-|S|`, `y=|S|-2alpha`, `z=2alpha-C`, and `w=C-M`.

On a common subsequence, write `Q_D/D->q`, `c_D/D->c`, `s_D/D->s_0`. Retained face currency gives

\[
2s_0+c+q\le z,\qquad q+c\le z.
\]

The exact §104 micro-route proof refines exceptional-micro capacity to `3z+6c`. Combining bounded nonquadrilateral boundary `5q`, nonprincipal routes `z+C_tail`, and the micro-route term gives at most `10z+C_tail`. Together with small-span and tooth terms,

\[
\boxed{B(x,y,z,w)\le Q_{tooth}(y)+y+{3\over2}x+10z+{35\over3}w.}
\]

The corrected companion's one-variable maximization is valid. At `sigma=0.0088`, independent recalculation gives

\[
A_*=0.4703752868004241\ldots,\quad
C_T=1.848752398466895\ldots,\quad
B_*=0.1162704799276074\ldots,
\]

and signed return forces normalized route excess

\[
0.0111063071953309\ldots>0.0088,
\]

with margin `0.0023063071953309...`. Therefore the strongest safe quantitative bound audited here is

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{diam}(P_*)\ge(0.9762439137425688\ldots-o(1))D.}
\]

This remains proof-only and noncanonical.

## §119 analytic envelope

§119's calculus is correct. Its older relaxation gives safe window `sigma<=0.0073` and coefficient `0.9762093528016385...`. It is quantitatively superseded by the corrected sharp §118 companion's `0.0088` window and should be read as a transparent analytic relaxation, not the numerical frontier.

## Actual remaining obligations

The full-core branch has two source-level structural formulations stronger than another decimal optimization.

### A. Outer-walk detour route

Use the exact §116 inequality

\[
d(L+P_{exc}-2d)\ge(4-2\sqrt3)A_4
\]

and canonical Oler/interface/global-minimality currencies to prove an incompatible upper bound on the same outer-walk detour, or show that a carrier with the required linear detour admits a source-preserving strict diameter decrease.

### B. Signed-return route

For one actual harmonic cell, prove a source-specific inequality

\[
\sum_{\tau\in\mathcal T_{gg}}(2-w_Q(u_\tau))>\sum_j(\ell_j-|z_j|)
\]

with complete physical trains, injective endpoint-to-route occurrence assignments, and every exceptional endpoint charged. §§117--118 establish this contradiction only in a bounded near-endpoint slack regime; general survivors may still have linear route excess and linear exceptional capacity.

Separately, the macroscopic inactive-container branch remains open. Its protected isolate/blocker structure has not forced an exact unit triangle, altered the old diameter-carrying core, or produced a same-cardinality strict sub-`D` source.

Those are the genuine C-II closing problems. Audit.44 remains the canonical state lock.
